Here are the three TUnit skip patterns:

**1. Skip a specific test method (attribute-based)**

```csharp
[Test]
[Skip("Waiting on payment-gateway sandbox provisioning")]
public async Task ChargeCard_ReturnsSuccess()
{
    // ...
}
```

**2. Skip an entire assembly conditionally**

```csharp
// In any file (typically AssemblyAttributes.cs)
#if SMOKE
[assembly: Skip("Assembly skipped – smoke configuration only runs smoke-tagged tests")]
#endif
```

**3. Runtime skip inside a test method**

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task SyncAccounts_PullsLatestData()
{
    if (!IsConnectedToCorporateVpn())
    {
        Assert.Skip("Machine is not joined to the corporate VPN");
    }

    // ... rest of test
}

private static bool IsConnectedToCorporateVpn()
{
    // e.g. check for a network interface or ping an internal host
    return System.Net.NetworkInformation.NetworkInterface
        .GetAllNetworkInterfaces()
        .Any(n => n.Name.Contains("CorpVPN") &&
                  n.OperationalStatus == System.Net.NetworkInformation.OperationalStatus.Up);
}
```

**Summary**

| Scenario | Mechanism | Scope |
|----------|-----------|-------|
| Known blocker | `[Skip("reason")]` on method | Single test |
| Build config | `[assembly: Skip("reason")]` with `#if` | Entire assembly |
| Runtime condition | `Assert.Skip("reason")` | Current test at execution time |

All three record the reason string in the test report so you can tell *why* a test was skipped.
